[{"source" ":org/Math-Academy" "forms" [{:db/id 17592186124635 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-37a7cf0c-c997-508e-b401-3de9aab0cfc4" "question-5b96b52d-bf1b-5917-8deb-641e04d4e427"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124634 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-4b9e706a-7ed7-5d91-bf85-9f21697ce716"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124641 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-9d9ea65f-7a2f-51be-a1e0-0db423dddcbc"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124642 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-bb3ee0d5-b81d-5d00-99b5-5160f6c70200"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-37a7cf0c-c997-508e-b401-3de9aab0cfc4" :question/id #uuid "37a7cf0c-c997-508e-b401-3de9aab0cfc4" :question/math-academy-id "q-159255" :question/problem "![](images/8a/8a50bd2b35b22d2ee71094467f1310f9c6c1510323e8106c13cf6fc45f2cbb9f.png)\n\nConsider the set $M=[1,3]\\cup [4,6]$ and the points $A,$ $B,$ and $C,$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $A$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n\nII. $B$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n\nIII. $C$ is an interior point of the set $M$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}\\in R$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is *smaller than* $\\delta :$ \n\n$$\n\\{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta \\}\n$$\n\nA neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is given by the open interval $({x}_{0}-\\delta ,{x}_{0}+\\delta ),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/f8/f889352f25e7936d0e5ea76585a979efbb301da61f7e99a937e979af93ee0664.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The point $A$ is *not* an interior point of $M$ since any neighborhood of $A$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\n![](images/0a/0a438012465859bc6406bb52a6bebeb13803760b429996fc7e4963b415c8f23d.png)\n\n- Statement II is true. The point $B$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $B$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n![](images/bb/bb374c99d9da28e40ad87dda6bf86dc6ca273a491e4faccb7dee043a104d0eba.png)\n\n- Statement III is false. The point $C$ is *not* an interior point of $M$ since any neighborhood of $C$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\n![](images/a5/a58fac3f854cd89429090b7fd10140d26d2097372586cd5f130353bff338b97c.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II only.'" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-710f49fa-4048-565e-bf85-ef63e229f768"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-710f49fa-4048-565e-bf85-ef63e229f768" :answer-field/id #uuid "710f49fa-4048-565e-bf85-ef63e229f768" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c641877a-fed0-5681-9ab6-c88e09b14311" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a07e75f8-1749-5ef7-a7ad-3f9a237b7f12" "answer-294aa78c-5727-5350-8592-0c1b6971026c" "answer-1761a7c3-ecef-5d09-a98b-e38fb750a0ca" "answer-14858488-0968-53ee-a271-6b2c706d24f6" "answer-c641877a-fed0-5681-9ab6-c88e09b14311"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a07e75f8-1749-5ef7-a7ad-3f9a237b7f12" :answer/id #uuid "a07e75f8-1749-5ef7-a7ad-3f9a237b7f12" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-294aa78c-5727-5350-8592-0c1b6971026c" :answer/id #uuid "294aa78c-5727-5350-8592-0c1b6971026c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1761a7c3-ecef-5d09-a98b-e38fb750a0ca" :answer/id #uuid "1761a7c3-ecef-5d09-a98b-e38fb750a0ca" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-14858488-0968-53ee-a271-6b2c706d24f6" :answer/id #uuid "14858488-0968-53ee-a271-6b2c706d24f6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c641877a-fed0-5681-9ab6-c88e09b14311" :answer/id #uuid "c641877a-fed0-5681-9ab6-c88e09b14311" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-5b96b52d-bf1b-5917-8deb-641e04d4e427" :question/id #uuid "5b96b52d-bf1b-5917-8deb-641e04d4e427" :question/math-academy-id "q-159273" :question/problem "![](images/d6/d60800fb2b986aac396a278599651b9ee116339d4b5bbab9a7a6ae5e16f6a3b2.png)\n\nConsider the set $M=(-2,0)\\cup \\{1\\}$ and the points $A,$ $B,$ and $C,$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $A$ is a boundary  point of the set $M$\n\nII. $B$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n\nIII. $C$ is an interior point of the set $M$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}\\in R$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is *smaller than* $\\delta :$ \n\n$$\n\\{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta \\}\n$$\n\nA neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is given by the open interval $({x}_{0}-\\delta ,{x}_{0}+\\delta ),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/f8/f889352f25e7936d0e5ea76585a979efbb301da61f7e99a937e979af93ee0664.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The point $A$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $A$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n![](images/b5/b57ab5bfd2f0d685e2918c2f62601b9439475e7f29e2fbdabb912c43411a829f.png)\n\n- Statement II is false. The point $B$ is *not* an interior point of $M$ since any neighborhood of $B$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\n![](images/f5/f5a2dc9c52c0c24b2f611cfd3b5aa23fb068249780fd66d80039b7d548c6151a.png)\n\n- Statement III is false. The point $C$ is *not* an interior point because every open neighborhood of $C$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n![](images/3b/3b47e92adb22c8d68cf94f947870d185ef907f9ca17c7427843e03fbcdab21cc.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I only.'" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ebcf0a61-7a0a-54a8-a956-1b1805e9c49e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ebcf0a61-7a0a-54a8-a956-1b1805e9c49e" :answer-field/id #uuid "ebcf0a61-7a0a-54a8-a956-1b1805e9c49e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d1bf783b-f62d-5544-9b6f-a82c84002604" :answer-field/choices ["answer-09cb1613-161a-586b-b39d-fa7423b2674b" "answer-e3286edb-fba7-50de-891f-e39e3ed3ca07" "answer-d1bf783b-f62d-5544-9b6f-a82c84002604" "answer-c478305f-33cc-5b5e-b111-aa60762b3eda" "answer-df46c36c-d040-5e4b-9960-bbaee7fd9b73"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-09cb1613-161a-586b-b39d-fa7423b2674b" :answer/id #uuid "09cb1613-161a-586b-b39d-fa7423b2674b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e3286edb-fba7-50de-891f-e39e3ed3ca07" :answer/id #uuid "e3286edb-fba7-50de-891f-e39e3ed3ca07" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d1bf783b-f62d-5544-9b6f-a82c84002604" :answer/id #uuid "d1bf783b-f62d-5544-9b6f-a82c84002604" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c478305f-33cc-5b5e-b111-aa60762b3eda" :answer/id #uuid "c478305f-33cc-5b5e-b111-aa60762b3eda" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-df46c36c-d040-5e4b-9960-bbaee7fd9b73" :answer/id #uuid "df46c36c-d040-5e4b-9960-bbaee7fd9b73" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-4b9e706a-7ed7-5d91-bf85-9f21697ce716" :question/id #uuid "4b9e706a-7ed7-5d91-bf85-9f21697ce716" :question/math-academy-id "q-64996" :question/problem "![](images/74/74bbb3351cf6720121c6ce0fbe8f99fa1c65560edbe5e547ecfd007835a50b89.png)\n\nConsider the set $M,$ depicted by the shaded region above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $A$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n\nII. $B$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n\nIII. $C$ is an interior point of the set $M$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n\\{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta \\}\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n![](images/17/173a69de844fac6141fe2fb3c695d070750b9df29b91364f66a0c1da28998428.png)\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n- Statement I is true. The point $A$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $A$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The point $B$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $B$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $C$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $C$ that is fully contained inside the set $M.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I, II, and III'." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b40b396f-afbe-53ce-beb3-0c52eeeedd6c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b40b396f-afbe-53ce-beb3-0c52eeeedd6c" :answer-field/id #uuid "b40b396f-afbe-53ce-beb3-0c52eeeedd6c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-75523642-a8e9-5c20-af07-5cbd062f55b0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d56409a1-dd3e-5129-b768-c2b620b4e283" "answer-f5e84243-54fd-5434-a031-9ceaa4099bad" "answer-75523642-a8e9-5c20-af07-5cbd062f55b0" "answer-13cc753c-b562-5cf3-bdc0-1f0bef4bdf5b" "answer-1fe93c47-e51a-5c46-96e7-9cb51c84cb58"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d56409a1-dd3e-5129-b768-c2b620b4e283" :answer/id #uuid "d56409a1-dd3e-5129-b768-c2b620b4e283" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f5e84243-54fd-5434-a031-9ceaa4099bad" :answer/id #uuid "f5e84243-54fd-5434-a031-9ceaa4099bad" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-75523642-a8e9-5c20-af07-5cbd062f55b0" :answer/id #uuid "75523642-a8e9-5c20-af07-5cbd062f55b0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-13cc753c-b562-5cf3-bdc0-1f0bef4bdf5b" :answer/id #uuid "13cc753c-b562-5cf3-bdc0-1f0bef4bdf5b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1fe93c47-e51a-5c46-96e7-9cb51c84cb58" :answer/id #uuid "1fe93c47-e51a-5c46-96e7-9cb51c84cb58" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-9d9ea65f-7a2f-51be-a1e0-0db423dddcbc" :question/id #uuid "9d9ea65f-7a2f-51be-a1e0-0db423dddcbc" :question/math-academy-id "q-158557" :question/problem "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:y\\le 2-x\\},\\,∂A=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:y=2-x\\}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The point $P(0,2)$ lies on the boundary of $A$\n\nII. The point $P(0,2)$ belongs to $A$\n\nIII. The point $Q(2,1)$ is an interior point of $A$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n\\{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta \\}\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie on or below the line $y=2-x.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/ab/ab319d348d61ba7d247b21553de0625f8c4f9906800102a6f8e11264472cab13.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(0,2)$ and $Q(2,1)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/9e/9ebf116e1093aa4861de907ed9256ec52e4a5b3e0765a6ab66cc8ee0b40e1769.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of $P(0,2)$ satisfy the equation $y=2-x.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $∂A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The coordinates of $P(0,2)$ satisfy the inequality $y\\le 2-x.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, $Q$ is not an interior point of $A$ since any neighborhood of $Q$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I and II only.'" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7274d4e1-605f-55f2-a572-987750313a00"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7274d4e1-605f-55f2-a572-987750313a00" :answer-field/id #uuid "7274d4e1-605f-55f2-a572-987750313a00" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-7c7d6717-571c-5bc0-bcde-f66a2294e7d7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7c7d6717-571c-5bc0-bcde-f66a2294e7d7" "answer-758ca936-3175-5b9a-a7f2-59300deea008" "answer-8b448c1a-f75e-567b-bba2-0c1b4f290c15" "answer-4a2b1785-6973-5d01-9c9d-ae0b2c5f9ef6" "answer-7933ca3c-2fce-5e9a-b5e4-ac56a04b4c95"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7c7d6717-571c-5bc0-bcde-f66a2294e7d7" :answer/id #uuid "7c7d6717-571c-5bc0-bcde-f66a2294e7d7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-758ca936-3175-5b9a-a7f2-59300deea008" :answer/id #uuid "758ca936-3175-5b9a-a7f2-59300deea008" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8b448c1a-f75e-567b-bba2-0c1b4f290c15" :answer/id #uuid "8b448c1a-f75e-567b-bba2-0c1b4f290c15" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4a2b1785-6973-5d01-9c9d-ae0b2c5f9ef6" :answer/id #uuid "4a2b1785-6973-5d01-9c9d-ae0b2c5f9ef6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7933ca3c-2fce-5e9a-b5e4-ac56a04b4c95" :answer/id #uuid "7933ca3c-2fce-5e9a-b5e4-ac56a04b4c95" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125216 :question/problem "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}\\le 1\\},\\,∂A=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=1\\}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The point $Q(-1,\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}})$ lies on the boundary of $A$\n\nII. The point $P(0.5,0.5)$ belongs to $A$\n\nIII. The point $Q(-1,\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}})$ is an interior point of $A$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$ \n\n$$\n\\{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta \\}\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie on or inside the ellipse $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}\\le 1.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/04/0485539c238417b1b93904f9a0e889198c64ff29a4629636094d33048d0bdd32.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(0.5,0.5)$ and $Q(-1,\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}})$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/04/0485539c238417b1b93904f9a0e889198c64ff29a4629636094d33048d0bdd32.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of $Q(-1,\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}})$ satisfy the equation $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=1.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $∂A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The coordinates of $P(0.5,0.5)$  satisfy the inequality $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}\\le 1.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Every open neighborhood of $Q$ contains points that belong to $A$ and points that do not belong to $A.$ The point $Q$ is a boundary point for the given set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I and II only.'" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186123896 :answer-field/correct 17592186123901 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-bb3ee0d5-b81d-5d00-99b5-5160f6c70200" :question/id #uuid "bb3ee0d5-b81d-5d00-99b5-5160f6c70200" :question/math-academy-id "q-158732" :question/problem "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:y>\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}\\},\\,∂A=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:y=\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The point $P(0,1)$ lies on the boundary of $A$\n\nII. The point $P(0,1)$ belongs to $A$\n\nIII. The point $Q(2,\\frac{4}{3})$ is an interior point of $A$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n\\{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta \\}\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nThe set $A$ consists of all points that lie to the left of the parabola ${y}^{2}=4x.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/8f/8f182873a8384097b56152284e67189e36f0a79a0b2bbda8926a2f8279db7ddd.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(0,1)$ and $Q(2,\\frac{4}{3})$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/66/66c8cb59cceadd43e3c4316925f1dd624c2e77a6ec547c9f9a1a02846bb537fb.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The coordinates of $P(0,1)$ do not satisfy the equation $y=\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}.$ Hence, $P$ does not lie on the boundary of the set.\n\n- Statement II is true. The coordinates of $P(0,1)$ satisfy the inequality $y>\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Every open neighborhood of $Q$ contains points that belong to $A$ and points that do not belong to $A.$ The point $Q$ is a boundary point for the given set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II only.'" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-374832d9-2085-56d7-b8ec-ffec23c86eb2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-374832d9-2085-56d7-b8ec-ffec23c86eb2" :answer-field/id #uuid "374832d9-2085-56d7-b8ec-ffec23c86eb2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-bfee3041-1ea5-5d62-91c3-245076039872" :answer-field/choices ["answer-98623bac-9b60-5778-9acc-c9d6970dc0f0" "answer-e6644f14-7427-5268-94b7-90f4b0379b06" "answer-3c23e6dc-98f3-56bf-9dec-3f31245a5e57" "answer-bfee3041-1ea5-5d62-91c3-245076039872" "answer-1db30fee-97ef-5c82-b9f7-e59f87e2d6b4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-98623bac-9b60-5778-9acc-c9d6970dc0f0" :answer/id #uuid "98623bac-9b60-5778-9acc-c9d6970dc0f0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e6644f14-7427-5268-94b7-90f4b0379b06" :answer/id #uuid "e6644f14-7427-5268-94b7-90f4b0379b06" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3c23e6dc-98f3-56bf-9dec-3f31245a5e57" :answer/id #uuid "3c23e6dc-98f3-56bf-9dec-3f31245a5e57" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bfee3041-1ea5-5d62-91c3-245076039872" :answer/id #uuid "bfee3041-1ea5-5d62-91c3-245076039872" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1db30fee-97ef-5c82-b9f7-e59f87e2d6b4" :answer/id #uuid "1db30fee-97ef-5c82-b9f7-e59f87e2d6b4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]}] "assertions" [["question-37a7cf0c-c997-508e-b401-3de9aab0cfc4" :question/id #uuid "37a7cf0c-c997-508e-b401-3de9aab0cfc4"] ["question-37a7cf0c-c997-508e-b401-3de9aab0cfc4" :question/math-academy-id "q-159255"] ["question-37a7cf0c-c997-508e-b401-3de9aab0cfc4" :question/problem "![](images/8a/8a50bd2b35b22d2ee71094467f1310f9c6c1510323e8106c13cf6fc45f2cbb9f.png)\n\nConsider the set $M=[1,3]\\cup [4,6]$ and the points $A,$ $B,$ and $C,$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $A$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n\nII. $B$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n\nIII. $C$ is an interior point of the set $M$"] ["question-37a7cf0c-c997-508e-b401-3de9aab0cfc4" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}\\in R$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is *smaller than* $\\delta :$ \n\n$$\n\\{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta \\}\n$$\n\nA neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is given by the open interval $({x}_{0}-\\delta ,{x}_{0}+\\delta ),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/f8/f889352f25e7936d0e5ea76585a979efbb301da61f7e99a937e979af93ee0664.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The point $A$ is *not* an interior point of $M$ since any neighborhood of $A$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\n![](images/0a/0a438012465859bc6406bb52a6bebeb13803760b429996fc7e4963b415c8f23d.png)\n\n- Statement II is true. The point $B$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $B$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n![](images/bb/bb374c99d9da28e40ad87dda6bf86dc6ca273a491e4faccb7dee043a104d0eba.png)\n\n- Statement III is false. The point $C$ is *not* an interior point of $M$ since any neighborhood of $C$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\n![](images/a5/a58fac3f854cd89429090b7fd10140d26d2097372586cd5f130353bff338b97c.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II only.'"] ["question-37a7cf0c-c997-508e-b401-3de9aab0cfc4" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate] ["answer-field-710f49fa-4048-565e-bf85-ef63e229f768" :answer-field/id #uuid "710f49fa-4048-565e-bf85-ef63e229f768"] ["answer-a07e75f8-1749-5ef7-a7ad-3f9a237b7f12" :answer/id #uuid "a07e75f8-1749-5ef7-a7ad-3f9a237b7f12"] ["answer-a07e75f8-1749-5ef7-a7ad-3f9a237b7f12" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-a07e75f8-1749-5ef7-a7ad-3f9a237b7f12" :answer/value "I and II only"] ["answer-29